1
00:00:19,327 --> 00:00:33,231
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I heard you talking the other day about saṃsāra and it not being the round rebirth or that wheel of time image.

2
00:00:33,331 --> 00:00:34,532
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you say that is?

3
00:00:34,552 --> 00:00:39,173
[SPEAKER_00]: You mentioned it was... the translation was something like wandering on.

4
00:00:39,613 --> 00:00:41,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, roaming, roaming on.

5
00:00:41,774 --> 00:00:42,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Saṃsārāti.

6
00:00:43,494 --> 00:00:44,915
[SPEAKER_01]: If I remember correctly, this means

7
00:00:45,933 --> 00:00:46,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Roaming.

8
00:00:46,414 --> 00:00:48,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Just keep roaming on and on and on and on.

9
00:00:49,736 --> 00:00:52,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Now here, then elsewhere.

10
00:00:54,220 --> 00:01:06,331
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the whole idea of like a round, of rebirth, it's a notion that people develop based on this life and based on the self-centeredness of this life.

11
00:01:07,436 --> 00:01:11,358
[SPEAKER_01]: By that, I mean, they use this life as a reference point.

12
00:01:12,438 --> 00:01:19,021
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, from there, you kind of die and sort of come back in some sort of cycle.

13
00:01:19,421 --> 00:01:27,184
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, fundamentally, if you look at the experience from the point of view of the five aggregates, there are no rounds.

14
00:01:27,504 --> 00:01:29,945
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just arising and passing away of the very same thing.

15
00:01:29,965 --> 00:01:33,507
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any rebirth that you might have, it's within the five aggregates.

16
00:01:34,453 --> 00:01:39,174
[SPEAKER_01]: So then... so that's why it's not even accurate to say, ah, it's the five aggregates that get reborn.

17
00:01:40,895 --> 00:01:45,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that would... that would kind of imply five aggregates traveling from one birth to...

18
00:01:45,396 --> 00:01:47,477
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no travel from one birth to the other.

19
00:01:48,297 --> 00:01:52,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Rebirth is within the five aggregates, as in reappearance, reemergence.

20
00:01:52,959 --> 00:01:57,000
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within the five aggregates that you have and can know right here and now.

21
00:01:57,840 --> 00:02:02,902
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why if somebody fully understands the nature of pañcakhandhā, they...

22
00:02:03,393 --> 00:02:06,274
[SPEAKER_01]: They fully understood the nature of rebirth, the nature of reappearance.

23
00:02:08,055 --> 00:02:22,523
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, if somebody remembers the previous life, one previous life, or five previous lives, or five hundred eons ago, the lifetimes, all they're going to remember is the five aggregates, or one among them.

24
00:02:24,564 --> 00:02:27,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that saṃsāra is the five aggregates.

25
00:02:28,453 --> 00:02:31,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Your experience as a whole, it's the container of the saṃsāra.

26
00:02:31,995 --> 00:02:39,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that, your point of view, your immediacy, the content of your senses, that can be different depending on the rebirth.

27
00:02:39,541 --> 00:02:49,628
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be more refined, it can be... it can be coarser, it can be unpleasant, it can be torturous, but overall, in its nature, the five aggregates is there.

28
00:02:49,848 --> 00:02:50,328
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

29
00:02:51,129 --> 00:02:56,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in a way, there is no, like, coming and going and cycling around the realm of

30
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:56,800
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

31
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:06,166
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole world, all of the realms, all the possible realms of your rebirth are within the five aggregates which you have right here and now.

32
00:03:07,127 --> 00:03:26,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the notion of rebirth, it's more accurately sort of reflected upon from the point of view of... like a change of rebirth would be change of immediacy, change of the degree of immediacy of your senses, the nature of your senses.

33
00:03:26,685 --> 00:03:33,068
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in terms of the nature of things, the nature of phenomena, nature of things to appear, there's no difference there.

34
00:03:33,088 --> 00:03:40,491
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why whenever they would talk about the rebirth in the suttas, it would be like, in a sense, almost like a memory.

35
00:03:40,511 --> 00:03:47,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you remember what you've done yesterday, you remember what you've done ten days ago, you remember what you've done ten years ago.

36
00:03:47,694 --> 00:03:50,635
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yes, some people might remember what they've done ten lifetimes ago.

37
00:03:51,448 --> 00:03:54,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the notion of you traveling from one river to the other, it's a notion based on the...

38
00:04:04,705 --> 00:04:10,989
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a projection of the external world, external point of view, independent of your experience as a whole.

39
00:04:11,489 --> 00:04:13,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a complete fallacy.

40
00:04:13,710 --> 00:04:23,856
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive things in such a purely impersonal manner, because that would require you to generally step outside of yourself, outside of the container of your five aggregates, outside of your point of view.

41
00:04:25,537 --> 00:04:29,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking something outside of your point of view, it's inconceivable.

42
00:04:29,599 --> 00:04:31,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever you can conceive, it's within your point of view.

43
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:33,781
[SPEAKER_01]: It's from your point of view.

44
00:04:33,801 --> 00:04:37,823
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point of view arises and ceases.

45
00:04:37,883 --> 00:04:39,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the point... Or changes.

46
00:04:40,424 --> 00:04:41,124
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose, yeah.

47
00:04:41,144 --> 00:04:46,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the degree of point of view can change within the container of the five aggregates.

48
00:04:48,848 --> 00:04:53,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, for example, with the animals, being the lower realm,

49
00:04:54,115 --> 00:05:03,299
[SPEAKER_01]: It means their point of view, their capacities of reflection are more forcefully absorbed with the content of the senses.

50
00:05:03,739 --> 00:05:10,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why they can't step back and become aware of their situation or they can do it to a very little degree.

51
00:05:10,742 --> 00:05:13,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Mostly, it's just absorption with immediacy.

52
00:05:13,763 --> 00:05:17,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Hunger, hunger, fear, fear, play, play, running, chasing, fighting.

53
00:05:18,722 --> 00:05:20,584
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't just... wait a minute, why am I doing this?

54
00:05:21,265 --> 00:05:23,747
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't have that much space in that reflection.

55
00:05:23,767 --> 00:05:25,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Their point of view is still absorbed.

56
00:05:26,110 --> 00:05:27,111
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still the point of view.

57
00:05:27,751 --> 00:05:31,215
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why somebody can remember the previous lifetimes.

58
00:05:31,235 --> 00:05:38,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's through these... through this container of the five aggregates that generates this point of view, you can actually remember more.

59
00:05:41,053 --> 00:05:48,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a life doesn't last long, but a point of view can last very long.

60
00:05:49,077 --> 00:05:52,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you know, in a way, that's true.

61
00:05:52,219 --> 00:06:00,323
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is, again, even when people think about the notion of time, they think in this external, scientific, impersonal sense.

62
00:06:00,443 --> 00:06:02,944
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's inconceivable.

63
00:06:02,964 --> 00:06:04,385
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, things are in time.

64
00:06:05,125 --> 00:06:08,607
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they can say the life lasts this much, and by the point of view, lasts forever.

65
00:06:09,316 --> 00:06:19,025
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the notion, any notion of eternity, in a sense of permanence, it's wrong, because it's founded in a wrong view.

66
00:06:19,766 --> 00:06:23,629
[SPEAKER_01]: And the wrong view is that which contradicts the nature of the phenomena, the way they appear.

67
00:06:23,929 --> 00:06:25,571
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't step outside of the five aggregates.

68
00:06:26,031 --> 00:06:35,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You assuming the world outside of your five aggregates, means you're basically having a wrong assumption in regard to the nature of the way that the five aggregates are.

69
00:06:36,367 --> 00:06:44,068
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could truly say that, if you want to be accurate, you can't say that five aggregates last for long, because that would imply that they are in time.

70
00:06:46,289 --> 00:06:52,050
[SPEAKER_01]: While, in reality, your notion of time is already within that container of the five aggregates, your experience as a whole.

71
00:06:52,970 --> 00:07:00,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, more accurately, you would say that five aggregates are not in time, five aggregates are off time.

72
00:07:01,411 --> 00:07:05,892
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, your notion of duration is already on the basis of enduring five aggregates.

73
00:07:09,418 --> 00:07:17,661
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, not so strictly speaking, you can say, yeah, it lasts... well, it lasts infinitely, but not eternally.

74
00:07:21,182 --> 00:07:24,324
[SPEAKER_00]: There's many different points of view.

75
00:07:24,384 --> 00:07:34,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I have a point of view now, here, and your point of view... Well, again, they would assume the external point of view, they could then compare all these different points of view.

76
00:07:34,628 --> 00:07:34,768
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

77
00:07:35,108 --> 00:07:36,248
[SPEAKER_01]: You have your point of view.

78
00:07:37,529 --> 00:07:38,529
[SPEAKER_00]: There is this point of view.

79
00:07:38,922 --> 00:07:39,282
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

80
00:07:39,562 --> 00:07:40,363
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you can know.

81
00:07:41,263 --> 00:07:50,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can come close to my point of view, but you can't have my point of view, because that would require you becoming me as an individual, and that's inconceivable.

82
00:07:51,408 --> 00:08:00,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there is internal point of view, the one that you know for yourself, and then you can see other points of view, such as external points of view.

83
00:08:00,693 --> 00:08:07,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can say there are points of view, but you can't see them on the same level, or you can't even compare them on the same level.

84
00:08:09,997 --> 00:08:14,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they require you to step outside of your point of view that you're inseparable from.

85
00:08:18,092 --> 00:08:26,300
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, what about development or, well, not exactly development, but evolution, this theory of evolution?

86
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:35,628
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I will continually evolve, evolve, my point of view will get better, naturally?

87
00:08:36,449 --> 00:08:39,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, no, no, of course not.

88
00:08:39,892 --> 00:08:41,994
[SPEAKER_01]: What evolves is not a point of view, it's the...

89
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:56,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what can be said to evolve or proliferate, depending on how you want to call it, is the senses and the world around the immediacy, around the content of your experience.

90
00:08:56,871 --> 00:09:09,822
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point of view is more on the level of how you regard that content, how you see it, how much you allow it to control you, to absorb you, to deprive you of your own capacity, of your own acts of reflection.

91
00:09:12,778 --> 00:09:21,982
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, point of view can... it's pretty much... well, it's indirectly affected by proliferation and evolution.

92
00:09:22,883 --> 00:09:32,007
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, it always maintains potential of stepping back and reflecting upon the immediacy of your current birth.

93
00:09:33,367 --> 00:09:35,048
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's these realms.

94
00:09:41,136 --> 00:09:47,782
[SPEAKER_00]: The animal realm, the human realm, and they're in between that.

95
00:09:47,802 --> 00:09:49,464
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't hear you, sorry.

96
00:09:49,484 --> 00:09:52,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the realms in between that, I think, maybe ghosts.

97
00:09:52,346 --> 00:09:52,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh-huh.

98
00:09:54,108 --> 00:09:57,251
[SPEAKER_00]: But there... there is no stepping.

99
00:09:58,171 --> 00:09:59,633
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no halfway realm.

100
00:10:00,293 --> 00:10:00,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, right.

101
00:10:00,634 --> 00:10:02,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there's no crossing between the realms.

102
00:10:02,816 --> 00:10:03,436
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you call it?

103
00:10:03,456 --> 00:10:04,257
[SPEAKER_00]: The missing link.

104
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:05,200
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

105
00:10:05,220 --> 00:10:11,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, any notion of comparison, connection, would require an external point of view.

106
00:10:12,065 --> 00:10:16,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Point of view that's independent of yourself, of your five aggregates, of your experience as a whole.

107
00:10:16,848 --> 00:10:17,849
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's inconceivable.

108
00:10:18,870 --> 00:10:23,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you know, phenomenologically speaking, is what you have.

109
00:10:24,674 --> 00:10:33,000
[SPEAKER_01]: As in a... Based on that, you can then infer that the immediacy and the senses and the sense engagement can be different, as in

110
00:10:33,450 --> 00:10:37,533
[SPEAKER_01]: It can... it can exercise a different extent, a different amount of force over you.

111
00:10:37,993 --> 00:10:46,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Like for animals, the senses exercise way too much force for the reflection to be able to maintain itself as much as a human can do.

112
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:50,260
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no crossing.

113
00:10:50,621 --> 00:11:00,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there is the animal realm that sits here, then there is the human realm that sits here, and then the being goes into animal realm, and then he kind of floats from animal into human or something like that.

114
00:11:01,149 --> 00:11:04,791
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like an externally projected notion of some sort of cosmology.

115
00:11:05,351 --> 00:11:12,175
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, if you're talking about it, there is no other way to talk about it than in these external terms, but those designations should not be misunderstood.

116
00:11:12,695 --> 00:11:16,698
[SPEAKER_01]: When it comes to your point of view, you can only see it from the inside.

117
00:11:18,399 --> 00:11:26,023
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how... that's how... that's how the Buddha would refer that, like, an arahant or somebody who is fully understood, understood the saṃsāra.

118
00:11:26,951 --> 00:11:29,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by traveling in previous lives or waiting for the future lives.

119
00:11:29,932 --> 00:11:31,153
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he understood the content.

120
00:11:31,593 --> 00:11:39,416
[SPEAKER_01]: He understood the... sorry, not the content, the container, the basis on account of any different content can manifest itself.

121
00:11:39,676 --> 00:11:44,618
[SPEAKER_01]: And by saying different content means different birth.

122
00:11:45,398 --> 00:11:55,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Content of an animal realm or content of a deva realm, as in the refinement of the content, the type of engagement of the content, that's basically what you can

123
00:11:56,936 --> 00:12:07,223
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't necessarily develop each birth.

124
00:12:07,963 --> 00:12:08,844
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not getting better.

125
00:12:10,265 --> 00:12:10,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, no, no.

126
00:12:10,805 --> 00:12:11,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

127
00:12:11,286 --> 00:12:11,506
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

128
00:12:12,587 --> 00:12:17,330
[SPEAKER_00]: You're getting worse, better, worse, better.

129
00:12:17,984 --> 00:12:28,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, based on your actions, they will kind of play the part into what kind of content you're going to be... your point of view will be paired with later on.

130
00:12:30,067 --> 00:12:34,708
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, again, you can... you don't have to think about previous life and future life.

131
00:12:34,748 --> 00:12:36,268
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the principle that you can extend.

132
00:12:36,608 --> 00:12:37,928
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can think within this life.

133
00:12:38,769 --> 00:12:45,490
[SPEAKER_01]: If the actions you're making now are quite unwholesome, harming a lot of people, for example, causing lots of damage,

134
00:12:47,507 --> 00:13:06,733
[SPEAKER_01]: You can safely assume that your whole life is heading in that direction, so then in five, six years, when the whole world is against you, they're trying to imprison you, they chase you because of the crimes you committed, your content has been significantly changed to... to like... like more pressing, more enduring, more unpleasant, because of your actions.

135
00:13:07,373 --> 00:13:08,813
[SPEAKER_01]: They've been affecting you in that manner.

136
00:13:09,913 --> 00:13:15,535
[SPEAKER_01]: If... if... if your actions are in this very life, are actually not harming anybody,

137
00:13:16,908 --> 00:13:18,069
[SPEAKER_01]: are helpful to everyone.

138
00:13:18,849 --> 00:13:22,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, yeah, that's going to be influencing your environment the most.

139
00:13:23,250 --> 00:13:30,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Not 100%, because there are many other factors that can play the part, but to a great deal.

140
00:13:30,693 --> 00:13:37,656
[SPEAKER_01]: If a person doesn't go around committing crimes, they don't have to worry about whether anybody's going to get them, catch them, exercise revenge on them.

141
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:49,430
[SPEAKER_01]: But those who do, that becomes your daily environment, that becomes your daily content of the phenomena that keeps appearing from your point of view, of the further actions you do on account of it.

142
00:13:50,191 --> 00:14:01,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could then see, if you carry on that kind of behavior for years, it's not a big stretch to assume that, oh yeah, so, when this life sort of breaks apart, when this body breaks apart,

143
00:14:02,502 --> 00:14:10,548
[SPEAKER_01]: The content that's going to re-arise, it's just going to carry on further in the same direction, because that's how you've been exercising your point of view and your actions and your intentions.

144
00:14:10,929 --> 00:14:17,974
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no, like, a natural end to saṃsāra, where you just get better, better, better, evolve in a good way, evolve, evolve.

145
00:14:18,315 --> 00:14:23,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in a way, it's, again, it's not like that you re-enter saṃsāra, you are the saṃsāra.

146
00:14:23,959 --> 00:14:26,702
[SPEAKER_01]: The five aggregates affected by ignorance is what saṃsāra is.

147
00:14:30,250 --> 00:14:44,257
[SPEAKER_01]: This, this experience of the five aggregates, for somebody who is not enlightened, that's, that's already what saṃsāra is, which means this experience is just gonna stay infinitely.

148
00:14:46,999 --> 00:14:53,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless a person frees this very experience from that ignorance, then it will not reappear.

149
00:14:54,582 --> 00:14:59,285
[SPEAKER_01]: The content will not... well, you won't be paired with any content, with any form.

150
00:15:00,116 --> 00:15:01,516
[SPEAKER_01]: That point of view will not be paired.

151
00:15:01,556 --> 00:15:05,757
[SPEAKER_01]: It will just go extinct, as the Buddha would describe nibbāna as extinction.

152
00:15:08,518 --> 00:15:09,558
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't go anywhere else.

153
00:15:10,258 --> 00:15:11,679
[SPEAKER_01]: It... well, it doesn't go anywhere else.

154
00:15:12,399 --> 00:15:21,841
[SPEAKER_01]: That would require you to jump from a point A in space to a point B in space, which then assumes the external point of view of space, which is only... can be based on the five aggregates right here, right now.

155
00:15:22,281 --> 00:15:25,201
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why your experience is not in space and in time.

156
00:15:25,682 --> 00:15:27,522
[SPEAKER_01]: It's off space and off time.

157
00:15:29,327 --> 00:15:35,350
[SPEAKER_01]: You get the notion of space based on your perceptions and notion of time pretty much based on your feelings and their endurance.

158
00:15:37,011 --> 00:15:38,132
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's secondary to it.

159
00:15:44,615 --> 00:15:54,941
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop thinking in terms of I'm in space and time, then the whole notion of rebirth becomes much more intelligible.

160
00:15:57,241 --> 00:16:05,608
[SPEAKER_01]: And it just fits perfectly with pretty much everything else that you can know right here and right now.

161
00:16:05,648 --> 00:16:07,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Point of view is beyond time.

162
00:16:07,970 --> 00:16:08,610
[SPEAKER_01]: It's off time.

163
00:16:08,890 --> 00:16:12,613
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not beyond time, because again, that assumes time, and now it's something beyond.

164
00:16:13,274 --> 00:16:15,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Notion of time, it's basically a result of the point of view.

165
00:16:19,679 --> 00:16:22,081
[SPEAKER_01]: And space.

166
00:16:22,121 --> 00:16:24,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Notion of time and space is a result of point of view.

167
00:16:24,943 --> 00:16:26,204
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a result of the five aggregates.

168
00:16:28,878 --> 00:16:32,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in a way, like, again, you can think of it on the level of this life.

169
00:16:32,801 --> 00:16:37,185
[SPEAKER_01]: So, say, you have... you... today is, you know, a new day.

170
00:16:37,625 --> 00:16:40,267
[SPEAKER_01]: You do... you do certain... you do certain things and so on.

171
00:16:40,407 --> 00:16:41,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you go to sleep.

172
00:16:42,389 --> 00:16:43,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you wake up.

173
00:16:45,211 --> 00:16:46,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, again, it's a new day.

174
00:16:46,972 --> 00:16:47,533
[SPEAKER_01]: You do things.

175
00:16:47,633 --> 00:16:49,794
[SPEAKER_01]: But, okay, you have a memory of yesterday and so on.

176
00:16:50,215 --> 00:16:51,676
[SPEAKER_01]: They will affect your choices today.

177
00:16:52,477 --> 00:16:53,758
[SPEAKER_01]: You have assumption of tomorrow.

178
00:16:53,978 --> 00:16:55,679
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to affect your choice today and so on.

179
00:16:57,431 --> 00:17:01,735
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's all within your experience as a whole, within your point of view, within the five aggregates.

180
00:17:02,736 --> 00:17:10,983
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be asking yourself, oh, was it me from yesterday, the same who became the one today, and is going to be the one tomorrow.

181
00:17:11,023 --> 00:17:14,326
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's just me who went to sleep yesterday, and now I woke up.

182
00:17:14,786 --> 00:17:21,352
[SPEAKER_01]: And in a way, it's a new day from a reference point of view, of yesterday, as in from a memory.

183
00:17:22,032 --> 00:17:24,515
[SPEAKER_01]: But all of that is within this experience today.

184
00:17:26,068 --> 00:17:26,849
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can extend.

185
00:17:27,189 --> 00:17:33,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Yesterday, ten years ago, ten lifetimes ago, you've just been waking up in different environments, so to speak.

186
00:17:34,235 --> 00:17:45,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why... oh, what does... what... the question of who gets reborn is a wrong question, because it assumes this external floating from one life to another, and what is it that it changes and goes there?

187
00:17:45,606 --> 00:17:53,872
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha wouldn't even answer it, because it's based on a completely wrong premise of this external world point of view, that it's kind of independent of you.

188
00:17:53,912 --> 00:18:04,480
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't possibly conceive anything like that, because if something... if you're conceiving something independent of you, you can only do so on account of depending it upon your experience of the five aggregates, which means it's not truly independent.

189
00:18:05,021 --> 00:18:06,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, it's a completely wrong view.

190
00:18:06,542 --> 00:18:14,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, any question that will come out of it, as in, who gets reborn from life number one to a life number two,

191
00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:23,342
[SPEAKER_01]: It just requires this non-existent external world, independent of your experience, independent of your sense of being and your point of view.

192
00:18:31,023 --> 00:18:32,584
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why, yeah, just ask yourself.

193
00:18:33,924 --> 00:18:36,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Yourself, that you can remember from ten years ago.

194
00:18:39,825 --> 00:18:40,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Who's that?

195
00:18:40,545 --> 00:18:44,346
[SPEAKER_01]: How did that person from ten years ago become you today?

196
00:18:45,414 --> 00:18:48,395
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize that's... well, that's not really a right question to formulate like that.

197
00:18:48,756 --> 00:18:56,719
[SPEAKER_01]: How did it... because you would assume that something has kind of traveled independent of you, from a point of view in the past, to a point of view right here.

198
00:18:57,259 --> 00:19:01,721
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, it's just... no, it's just... this point of... this experience has been enduring for 10 years.

199
00:19:02,422 --> 00:19:07,204
[SPEAKER_01]: There have been changes on a particular level, while overall it was remaining the same container.

200
00:19:07,824 --> 00:19:22,079
[SPEAKER_01]: The same nature of perception... You've been having different perceptions, different intentions, you've been performing different actions ten years ago, because you had different values and different views, but overall, it was still the nature of perception, the nature of feeling, the nature of intention, the nature of the views.

201
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:24,241
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's within this.

202
00:19:24,261 --> 00:19:26,023
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, oh, you can't step outside of that.

203
00:19:27,445 --> 00:19:28,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahant doesn't step outside of it.

204
00:19:29,387 --> 00:19:30,488
[SPEAKER_01]: He actually realizes that

205
00:19:32,133 --> 00:19:35,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Outside is inconceivable, so he stops conceiving it.

206
00:19:35,636 --> 00:19:43,282
[SPEAKER_01]: And by doing so, he removes ignorance in regard to the inside of himself, in regard to the nature of perception, feeling, intention, the nature of the view.

207
00:19:44,322 --> 00:19:47,124
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how he extinguishes that container.

208
00:19:47,845 --> 00:19:51,328
[SPEAKER_01]: It ceases to be the fertile ground for this maintenance of the being.

209
00:19:53,269 --> 00:19:55,791
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't assume outside of the experience.

210
00:19:56,331 --> 00:19:57,132
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

211
00:19:58,033 --> 00:20:00,715
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's... Well, even Sotāpanna stops assuming that.

212
00:20:01,270 --> 00:20:12,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the fundamental, the core of the external assumption of your own experience is attavāda, the external sense of self, pretty much, independent of this experience and everything.

213
00:20:12,418 --> 00:20:14,619
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they would ask, who gets reborn?

214
00:20:15,660 --> 00:20:23,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they assume the impersonal world, the impersonal space and time from which things just flow, from one birth to the other realm, to this realm.

215
00:20:25,586 --> 00:20:28,428
[SPEAKER_01]: But, no matter the realm, the nature of the five aggregates is the same.

216
00:20:29,089 --> 00:20:30,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, oh, so if you want to find

217
00:20:31,119 --> 00:20:41,084
[SPEAKER_01]: How it is in more subtle realms or coarser realms, you can do so on account of understanding the nature of this experience that you have, because it's within it.

218
00:20:43,045 --> 00:20:45,046
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what... and that's why it's called roaming onwards.

219
00:20:45,467 --> 00:20:49,949
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just this experience just keeps perpetuating itself on and on and on.

220
00:20:50,569 --> 00:20:53,291
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha, again, he did give a simile that illustrates that.

221
00:20:53,331 --> 00:20:56,933
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, it's like a man leaving his home village and then goes to the next village.

222
00:20:57,313 --> 00:20:59,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, from that village, he goes to the next village.

223
00:20:59,154 --> 00:21:00,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, from that village, he goes to the next village.

224
00:21:01,787 --> 00:21:05,211
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, at 20th village, they ask him, where did you come from?

225
00:21:05,852 --> 00:21:07,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, well, before this village, I was in this village.

226
00:21:07,894 --> 00:21:10,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Before that, I was in another village, and another village, and another village.

227
00:21:10,878 --> 00:21:15,383
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, if you were to describe it to somebody else, it would sound like that external manner.

228
00:21:15,403 --> 00:21:19,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But from his point of view, he was always in the village that he was in.

229
00:21:21,508 --> 00:21:30,614
[SPEAKER_01]: He couldn't step outside of himself and then explain to himself how he didn't need to, how he from one village crossed to another village.

230
00:21:30,714 --> 00:21:33,856
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it was just, I was in this village, then I went on to another village.

231
00:21:34,616 --> 00:21:35,997
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still the same experience.

232
00:21:36,417 --> 00:21:39,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Each time he was in different village, he had a different content of a village.

233
00:21:41,080 --> 00:21:48,245
[SPEAKER_01]: But in terms of the nature of his experience, perception of the village, intentions of the village, feelings in the village were the same.

234
00:21:49,180 --> 00:21:51,601
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature remained the same, the nature of the five aggregates.

235
00:21:51,922 --> 00:21:52,702
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha meant.

236
00:21:52,722 --> 00:22:00,347
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, even if you remember 500 lifetimes more from before, all you're remembering is these five aggregates right here, right now, or one among them.

237
00:22:01,147 --> 00:22:02,448
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within this.

238
00:22:02,488 --> 00:22:16,977
[SPEAKER_01]: You said, you cannot... you cannot know the five aggregates directly, or you cannot... you assume them... Well, you cannot examine them and take them apart again, because that would require the external point of view in regard to them.

239
00:22:17,017 --> 00:22:18,498
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only see the insides.

240
00:22:19,513 --> 00:22:20,453
[SPEAKER_01]: of the five aggregates.

241
00:22:21,153 --> 00:22:26,515
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you think, oh, this is the inside, you already start implying the assumption, the conceiving of the outside.

242
00:22:27,075 --> 00:22:27,795
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha meant.

243
00:22:27,815 --> 00:22:31,376
[SPEAKER_01]: He conceives in matter, from matter, apart from matter.

244
00:22:31,656 --> 00:22:33,056
[SPEAKER_01]: He conceives matter is mine.

245
00:22:33,476 --> 00:22:37,937
[SPEAKER_01]: He conceives... and then he goes on in that Mūla Pāryāya Sutta for all the other aggregates and all the other ways.

246
00:22:39,277 --> 00:22:46,939
[SPEAKER_01]: By implicitly perceiving it as, ah, this is the inside, you automatically assume the outside, to an extent.

247
00:22:48,265 --> 00:22:53,569
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just have to undo that implicit conceiving and then the assumption of the outside will fade.

248
00:22:55,951 --> 00:22:57,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Undo the implicit conceiving?

249
00:22:57,772 --> 00:22:58,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

250
00:23:05,598 --> 00:23:14,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And you, again, you undo that implicit conceiving not on account of finding out the external view of the five aggregates and then examining it.

251
00:23:16,293 --> 00:23:16,793
[SPEAKER_01]: One by one.

252
00:23:16,913 --> 00:23:17,773
[SPEAKER_01]: That's inconceivable.

253
00:23:18,493 --> 00:23:19,214
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

254
00:23:21,034 --> 00:23:25,215
[SPEAKER_01]: You undo it by stop regarding it as mine, right here, right now.

255
00:23:29,156 --> 00:23:30,636
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing that it's independent of you.

256
00:23:31,356 --> 00:23:34,257
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing that it's subjected to destruction.

257
00:23:34,297 --> 00:23:34,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Inaccessible.

258
00:23:35,217 --> 00:23:36,077
[SPEAKER_01]: That it's inaccessible.

259
00:23:36,337 --> 00:23:36,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

260
00:23:36,577 --> 00:23:40,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were truly able to access it and control it, it would obey you.

261
00:23:40,458 --> 00:23:41,018
[SPEAKER_01]: It would listen you.

262
00:23:41,078 --> 00:23:41,718
[SPEAKER_01]: It would go with you.

263
00:23:44,080 --> 00:23:45,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

264
00:23:45,701 --> 00:23:54,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, like some people, some minds can go to the subtle level of being mindful of the earth element.

265
00:23:55,268 --> 00:24:02,453
[SPEAKER_01]: But, whether you're mindful of the earth element, whether you're mindful of the body seated on this chair, it's, it's the same principle.

266
00:24:04,034 --> 00:24:09,017
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you can arrive at the same goal, without needing to refine it to the point of the earth element.

267
00:24:12,735 --> 00:24:29,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Being mindful of your experience as a whole, bound up with this body, with this form, without overdoing your thought into assuming the external world point of view, but without becoming negligent in regard to your own absorption with your own senses.

268
00:24:34,461 --> 00:24:40,923
[SPEAKER_01]: So again, if a person wants to reflect along these lines, they can within this... with experience and the memories of this life.

269
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:42,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

270
00:24:42,600 --> 00:24:46,663
[SPEAKER_01]: If I ask you, where were you, I don't know, five days ago, you say, I was there.

271
00:24:46,703 --> 00:24:47,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Where were you ten years ago?

272
00:24:47,983 --> 00:24:48,464
[SPEAKER_01]: I was there.

273
00:24:49,724 --> 00:24:54,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in a way, yeah, there was some change, but within your point of view hasn't changed.

274
00:24:55,608 --> 00:25:02,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Your views might have changed, but the direction of you having views, the experience as a whole, the nature of perception, the nature of body,

275
00:25:03,597 --> 00:25:09,240
[SPEAKER_01]: The form, the nature of feeling, the nature of perception, the nature of intention, the nature of consciousness, that hasn't changed.

276
00:25:09,260 --> 00:25:12,742
[SPEAKER_01]: That container has remained unchanged since you can remember.

277
00:25:13,083 --> 00:25:13,723
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the thing.

278
00:25:15,364 --> 00:25:17,765
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remember since when you were two years old.

279
00:25:18,346 --> 00:25:19,867
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's kind of where it stopped.

280
00:25:19,887 --> 00:25:22,088
[SPEAKER_01]: But it only stops because your memory stops there.

281
00:25:22,128 --> 00:25:23,329
[SPEAKER_01]: But the principle carries on.

282
00:25:23,689 --> 00:25:26,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means then, yes, some people can then start remembering more.

283
00:25:27,091 --> 00:25:30,353
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still within the same principle of the memory that you know.

284
00:25:32,108 --> 00:25:42,676
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, it's not correct to say that it's, oh, yeah, it's my sense of self that got reborn, or it was previously in this life, now it's in this life, then it will go in another life.

285
00:25:42,696 --> 00:25:42,836
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

286
00:25:43,116 --> 00:25:44,557
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in space and time.

287
00:25:45,958 --> 00:25:46,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's memory?

288
00:25:46,839 --> 00:25:48,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Where does memory sit?

289
00:25:49,120 --> 00:25:50,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Where does memory sit?

290
00:25:50,121 --> 00:25:59,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it sits in that infinity of change on a more particular level, within the container that stays the same.

291
00:25:59,858 --> 00:26:03,780
[SPEAKER_01]: The container of your experience as a whole, the container of the five aggregates, the container of your point of view.

292
00:26:05,401 --> 00:26:07,203
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why memory is, in a way, endless.

293
00:26:08,143 --> 00:26:08,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, beginningless.

294
00:26:12,766 --> 00:26:15,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there was no first beginning, before which it didn't exist, and then you became.

295
00:26:16,708 --> 00:26:20,771
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, Avijja has no first point, when it manifests.

296
00:26:21,552 --> 00:26:26,955
[SPEAKER_01]: It shouldn't be there, but it is there, and its being there is beginningless.

297
00:26:28,056 --> 00:26:35,439
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why once you undo it, cannot re-arise, cannot reappear, cannot re-emerge, because it was never meant to be there.

298
00:26:37,140 --> 00:26:40,921
[SPEAKER_01]: The ignorance, the assumption of the external point of view, all of that, was not meant to be there.

299
00:26:41,782 --> 00:26:42,502
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't fit there.

300
00:26:43,402 --> 00:26:51,946
[SPEAKER_01]: The upādāna is not the five aggregates, cannot be the five aggregates, cannot become the five aggregates, cannot enter the five aggregates, but cannot be found apart from them.

301
00:26:54,620 --> 00:27:01,885
[SPEAKER_01]: So, desire and lust, which is another way of saying, misconceiving the five aggregates, the upādāna, the assumptions are there in regard to it.

302
00:27:02,285 --> 00:27:04,687
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop misconceiving it, the upādāna fades.

303
00:27:07,869 --> 00:27:10,090
[SPEAKER_00]: And so does the one doing none.

304
00:27:10,611 --> 00:27:10,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

305
00:27:11,131 --> 00:27:16,815
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no more being bound with the content like this, then like that, then another one, then another one.

306
00:27:16,835 --> 00:27:16,895
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

307
00:27:21,742 --> 00:27:29,148
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what generates saṃsāra is ignorance.

308
00:27:29,448 --> 00:27:30,109
[SPEAKER_01]: What generates?

309
00:27:30,509 --> 00:27:31,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe not the right word.

310
00:27:32,491 --> 00:27:33,252
[SPEAKER_00]: What keeps it going.

311
00:27:33,452 --> 00:27:33,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

312
00:27:34,052 --> 00:27:35,393
[SPEAKER_01]: What maintains saṃsāra?

313
00:27:36,534 --> 00:27:38,216
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't have that...

314
00:27:38,236 --> 00:27:40,418
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't have ignorance, saṃsāra cannot be maintained.

315
00:27:44,669 --> 00:27:48,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Just back to the memory, you're saying, where does it sit?

316
00:27:48,933 --> 00:27:54,599
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a mental... Again, where does it sit implies placement in space.

317
00:27:55,159 --> 00:27:58,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's over here.

318
00:27:59,843 --> 00:28:05,208
[SPEAKER_01]: But that assumes the external point of view, where you can place it, like a box on a shelf or something.

319
00:28:05,288 --> 00:28:05,769
[SPEAKER_00]: In space.

320
00:28:06,187 --> 00:28:06,808
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

321
00:28:06,828 --> 00:28:08,669
[SPEAKER_01]: At most, that can just be a figure of speech.

322
00:28:09,390 --> 00:28:14,754
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if that figure of speech is taken literally, as in, then you start assuming that it's in the space.

323
00:28:15,675 --> 00:28:16,395
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in the brain.

324
00:28:16,756 --> 00:28:21,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's in the brain, it's in a center or in some molecules there or something.

325
00:28:21,900 --> 00:28:22,060
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

326
00:28:22,280 --> 00:28:26,884
[SPEAKER_01]: It's no more in your brain that it's more in your toes.

327
00:28:27,945 --> 00:28:30,047
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, consciousness is not in your body.

328
00:28:31,888 --> 00:28:32,929
[SPEAKER_01]: It's paired with the body.

329
00:28:33,029 --> 00:28:34,130
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in regard to the body.

330
00:28:35,547 --> 00:28:43,031
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, any notions that you have on account... any notions that you have of your body, it's on the basis of conscious body.

331
00:28:43,852 --> 00:28:51,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Any notions that you have on account of... any notions that you have in regard to consciousness, it's on the basis of the conscious body.

332
00:28:51,917 --> 00:28:59,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Conscious body is the basis that you cannot step outside of, nor separate and investigate separately.

333
00:29:00,682 --> 00:29:02,963
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why whenever you look at the body, you're conscious.

334
00:29:03,407 --> 00:29:07,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever you're trying to discern consciousness, the necessary living body needs to be there.

335
00:29:08,889 --> 00:29:19,692
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is when people look at either body of consciousness and then develop the view that places them underneath or rather as more fundamental than the dyad of conscious body that you have right here, right now.

336
00:29:20,732 --> 00:29:22,033
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't step outside of it.

337
00:29:22,553 --> 00:29:28,455
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you have to realize that no... no... doesn't matter how pleasing the notions of the body you might develop,

338
00:29:29,373 --> 00:29:34,175
[SPEAKER_01]: And the theories and the views, that can only be done within the container of conscious body.

339
00:29:34,535 --> 00:29:35,836
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot step outside of it.

340
00:29:36,456 --> 00:29:38,657
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why scientific view, it's inherently wrong.

341
00:29:39,657 --> 00:29:42,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Not wrong in terms of the content, but wrong in terms of the nature.

342
00:29:43,399 --> 00:29:50,482
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you cannot use science to arrive and explain the nature of being, the nature of experience, the nature of feelings, for that matter.

343
00:29:53,563 --> 00:29:58,745
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why whenever the scientists try to talk about feelings and describe feelings, they end up just describing

344
00:30:00,624 --> 00:30:02,805
[SPEAKER_01]: perceptions of sensations in the body.

345
00:30:03,986 --> 00:30:06,627
[SPEAKER_01]: But perception is perception and feeling is feeling.

346
00:30:06,647 --> 00:30:07,988
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't feel your perceptions.

347
00:30:12,010 --> 00:30:16,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, perceptions and feelings in the same sense like body and consciousness.

348
00:30:16,652 --> 00:30:20,014
[SPEAKER_01]: They're paired, but they're independent of each other, but they're inseparable from each other.

349
00:30:20,314 --> 00:30:21,055
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said that.

350
00:30:21,075 --> 00:30:21,875
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't...

351
00:30:21,895 --> 00:30:26,638
[SPEAKER_01]: He said perception, feeling and intentions are conjoined.

352
00:30:26,918 --> 00:30:28,759
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot take them apart and examine them

353
00:30:29,705 --> 00:30:30,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Separate from the other two.

354
00:30:33,208 --> 00:30:39,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Means that's the basis that you cannot conceive different in any other shape or form.

355
00:30:41,214 --> 00:30:44,677
[SPEAKER_01]: And any conceivings you have, it's within that container of that basis.

356
00:30:45,878 --> 00:30:51,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, if your conceivings are contradicting the basis, it's a wrong view.

357
00:30:51,122 --> 00:30:52,223
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a wrong view.

358
00:30:55,750 --> 00:31:00,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why if you were to have a scientific view in terms of practicing the dhamma, it's a wrong view.

359
00:31:01,875 --> 00:31:07,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it contradicts the nature of the basis on account of which you developed the scientific view.

360
00:31:09,160 --> 00:31:21,209
[SPEAKER_01]: That external world that you cannot actually conceive truly, you cannot access in that impersonal manner as the science leads you to believe.

361
00:31:22,750 --> 00:31:23,071
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

362
00:31:23,191 --> 00:31:24,672
[SPEAKER_01]: You always have a point of view.

363
00:31:25,453 --> 00:31:28,959
[SPEAKER_01]: And the notion of, no, this is beyond the point of view, is within your point of view.

364
00:31:29,521 --> 00:31:30,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, this is external to my point of view.

365
00:31:30,983 --> 00:31:33,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can only see that through your point of view, which means it's not external.

366
00:31:34,930 --> 00:31:38,457
[SPEAKER_01]: The true external... Well, see, you can't even designate it correctly.

367
00:31:39,473 --> 00:31:43,375
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say the true external, well, it's not external then, because you're talking about it.

368
00:31:43,395 --> 00:31:50,418
[SPEAKER_01]: You're designating something, and if there is something, even an ambiguous notion of external world, it's within this container of the five aggregates.

369
00:31:50,498 --> 00:31:51,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's not truly external.

370
00:31:51,959 --> 00:31:56,941
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why there is no real matter, a real reality behind these appearances, or anything like that.

371
00:31:57,901 --> 00:31:59,322
[SPEAKER_01]: What appears, that's what's real.

372
00:32:02,454 --> 00:32:10,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, like I should speak about God, like a God is like this... Sure, sure, sure.

373
00:32:10,058 --> 00:32:13,880
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're conceiving it through, through labeling it, through designating how it's inconceivable.

374
00:32:14,380 --> 00:32:16,582
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot express the deity, the eternal.

375
00:32:16,682 --> 00:32:20,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you just expressed it, by saying it's, it's not expressible.

376
00:32:21,844 --> 00:32:24,346
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter what you express, it is within.

377
00:32:24,546 --> 00:32:25,406
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, yep, yep.

378
00:32:26,187 --> 00:32:30,229
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's, you realize, you can't step outside of your, of the experience as a whole.

379
00:32:30,489 --> 00:32:31,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And that was never the point.

380
00:32:32,272 --> 00:32:39,605
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you stop looking for some hidden transcendental realities, which is what usually think... what usually people think Arahantship is.

381
00:32:40,246 --> 00:32:45,015
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you realize, you can't... you... all you have to do is stop conceiving that there is outside to be found.

382
00:32:46,441 --> 00:32:51,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Because conceiving the outside is conceiving the independence of this experience as a whole.

383
00:32:51,605 --> 00:33:01,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a definition of atavāda, an independent sense of self, independent of the five aggregates, independent of the form, feeling, perceptions, intentions and consciousness.

384
00:33:02,633 --> 00:33:09,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, any notion of that independence is based on this form, feeling, perception, intentions, consciousness.

385
00:33:09,998 --> 00:33:12,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's actually secondary to it.

386
00:33:12,180 --> 00:33:13,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it cannot be independent.

387
00:33:14,645 --> 00:33:16,006
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's all a person needs to do.

388
00:33:16,466 --> 00:33:17,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop assuming the external.

389
00:33:18,427 --> 00:33:20,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop assuming that master self.

390
00:33:23,770 --> 00:33:25,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop conceiving it as possible.

391
00:33:28,952 --> 00:33:30,733
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's Mūla Parīyā's sutta in a nutshell.

392
00:33:32,694 --> 00:33:34,275
[SPEAKER_00]: The very first sutta in Lajjanaikāra.

393
00:33:36,136 --> 00:33:43,500
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea that before anything there was nothing.

394
00:33:43,540 --> 00:33:43,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

395
00:33:45,577 --> 00:33:48,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's something, because you're thinking of it.

396
00:33:48,639 --> 00:33:56,043
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if something is truly nothing, then you can't even designate it as nothing.

397
00:33:56,083 --> 00:33:58,004
[SPEAKER_01]: As absence of something, it's just inconceivable.

398
00:33:58,064 --> 00:34:02,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you can't even think it, and let alone designate it as something.

399
00:34:03,447 --> 00:34:07,629
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you keep trying to think it, means you just keep trying to contradict yourself, your own experience.

400
00:34:08,429 --> 00:34:11,171
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you shouldn't think it.

401
00:34:11,851 --> 00:34:13,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you should realize that it's unthinkable.

402
00:34:16,202 --> 00:34:21,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And the projection towards thinking it, you realize, well, that's already thinkable within this.

403
00:34:21,763 --> 00:34:24,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it cannot stand for that which is truly unthinkable.

404
00:34:25,304 --> 00:34:36,327
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the thought of unthinkable stops conceiving something that it's thinkable, that's when the thought recognizes its own extent.

405
00:34:45,602 --> 00:34:49,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Some philosophers speak about being and nothingness.

406
00:34:50,383 --> 00:35:01,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yes, but the ones that do, ended up assuming the external world from which they very plausibly defined being and nothingness.

407
00:35:04,987 --> 00:35:09,008
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's already then contradicting the basic phenomenological principle that they used to arrive at it.

408
00:35:11,464 --> 00:35:21,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from that sutta, Madhyamikāya 1, it says, a person can experience nibbāna, but he assumes nibbāna.

409
00:35:21,231 --> 00:35:21,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

410
00:35:21,852 --> 00:35:35,422
[SPEAKER_01]: He can have the experience of absence, of greed, aversion, delusion, but he can have it affected by the assumption of external sense of self, external master, external point of view, that there's external scientific order.

411
00:35:36,343 --> 00:35:36,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus,

412
00:35:37,073 --> 00:35:45,017
[SPEAKER_01]: That experience of not-self, that experience of freedom from greed, aversion, delusion, is appropriated by that fully gratuitous view.

413
00:35:45,677 --> 00:35:47,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, he conceives that nibbāna.

414
00:35:47,678 --> 00:35:48,498
[SPEAKER_01]: He conceives in it.

415
00:35:48,558 --> 00:35:50,639
[SPEAKER_01]: He conceives apart from it.

416
00:35:50,799 --> 00:35:51,860
[SPEAKER_01]: He conceives it as mine.

417
00:35:52,980 --> 00:35:55,061
[SPEAKER_01]: And thus, he cannot steady his mind.

418
00:35:55,762 --> 00:36:05,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because of that, that nibbāna cannot become the experience that he develops fully, consistently.

419
00:36:06,669 --> 00:36:07,669
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is the nibbāna.

420
00:36:08,149 --> 00:36:18,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is another sutta in Majjhima Nikāra, later on, when the Buddha gives a similar description how a person with his conceit thinks, oh, this is nibbāna, I'm attaining nibbāna, this nibbāna is mine.

421
00:36:19,473 --> 00:36:22,814
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, yeah, what he refers to is the actual nibbāna.

422
00:36:23,554 --> 00:36:29,616
[SPEAKER_01]: But because of the self-centered point of view, because of those assumptions of self and mastery, he does not enter that nibbāna.

423
00:36:31,577 --> 00:36:32,377
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot sustain it.

424
00:36:36,908 --> 00:36:38,669
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a different nibbāna.

425
00:36:39,450 --> 00:36:39,970
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a cop-out.

426
00:36:40,170 --> 00:36:41,951
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like when people try, oh, no, it's a different nibbāna.

427
00:36:41,971 --> 00:36:43,993
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just an easy way to dismiss it.

428
00:36:44,353 --> 00:36:53,479
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Buddha himself said, although he asserts that which is nibbāna, he doesn't enter it because he can't study his experience in it.

429
00:36:53,659 --> 00:36:55,420
[SPEAKER_01]: He keeps misconsuming it.

430
00:36:55,480 --> 00:37:02,105
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of death being the end, the ending of things, from what you were saying,

431
00:37:03,632 --> 00:37:06,615
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't seem to be the end.

432
00:37:06,655 --> 00:37:17,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Death, it's the end of the assumptions that you developed based on the familiarity with the current content of your experience.

433
00:37:19,626 --> 00:37:20,767
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, it's the end of that.

434
00:37:21,747 --> 00:37:25,230
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's far from the end of the five aggregates.

435
00:37:25,470 --> 00:37:26,752
[SPEAKER_01]: The true end of being.

436
00:37:27,552 --> 00:37:28,753
[SPEAKER_01]: It's far from the end of being.

437
00:37:33,268 --> 00:37:42,390
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, most people, the entire life, the sense of self, the views, are based on the content of this present experience, on that assumption of the external world and that whole thing.

438
00:37:42,970 --> 00:37:44,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, yeah, death ends all that.

439
00:37:45,270 --> 00:37:46,290
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's so frightening.

440
00:37:49,951 --> 00:37:53,631
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to... Yeah, if you stop...

441
00:37:53,952 --> 00:38:02,253
[SPEAKER_01]: If you removed all the conceivings and stopped wrongly assuming things in regard to this content of this experience, this life,

442
00:38:04,176 --> 00:38:06,518
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no death, hence the deathless.

443
00:38:07,778 --> 00:38:13,842
[SPEAKER_01]: That which is death can apply only where the content, where the assumptions in regard to the content of your senses exist.

444
00:38:14,443 --> 00:38:16,784
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the assumptions, that is the true deathless.

445
00:38:17,125 --> 00:38:21,728
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the notion of death is inconceivable.

446
00:38:21,748 --> 00:38:27,572
[SPEAKER_00]: That's almost what a lot of people think that death is, you know, the complete, some ending.

447
00:38:28,642 --> 00:38:35,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again... See, the fact that you can think, that you can have a notion of complete nothingness, well, that's something.

448
00:38:35,944 --> 00:38:39,925
[SPEAKER_01]: That notion is real as such, which means it's not the true nothingness.

449
00:38:41,685 --> 00:38:44,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it's a true nothingness, you couldn't think it.

450
00:38:44,486 --> 00:38:51,267
[SPEAKER_01]: But the non-conceiving death... Non-conceiving death means stop assuming wrong things in regard to the nature of death.

451
00:38:51,987 --> 00:38:53,508
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what non-conceiving death means.

452
00:38:53,548 --> 00:38:53,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbāna.

453
00:38:54,668 --> 00:38:55,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

454
00:38:57,605 --> 00:39:01,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can have an understanding of what nibbāna is.

455
00:39:02,049 --> 00:39:02,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

456
00:39:03,170 --> 00:39:03,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop it.

457
00:39:10,656 --> 00:39:11,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Without assuming.

458
00:39:12,718 --> 00:39:15,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what development is.

459
00:39:15,920 --> 00:39:16,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Undoing the assumptions.

460
00:39:17,422 --> 00:39:18,302
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't see in it.

461
00:39:18,442 --> 00:39:19,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you see nibbāna?

462
00:39:19,643 --> 00:39:19,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

463
00:39:20,024 --> 00:39:21,165
[SPEAKER_01]: You can enter it.

464
00:39:22,146 --> 00:39:23,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You can step into it, so to speak.

465
00:39:24,579 --> 00:39:28,882
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't see it, externally examine it, because if you are seeing it like that, you're misconceiving it.

466
00:39:30,063 --> 00:39:37,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can enter nibbāna by removing all the assumptions that misconceive what nibbāna is.

467
00:39:38,670 --> 00:39:41,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, your experience automatically becomes experience of nibbāna.

468
00:39:43,693 --> 00:39:49,177
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not like you do the work of removing the assumptions, and then the gates open, and then you walk into the nibbāna.

469
00:39:49,638 --> 00:39:49,778
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

470
00:39:49,898 --> 00:39:53,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Undoing the work, removing all the conceiving, is what nibbāna is.

471
00:39:56,901 --> 00:39:57,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it missed again?

472
00:40:21,673 --> 00:40:26,977
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a way, you are striving to bring about the ending being.

473
00:40:26,997 --> 00:40:27,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

474
00:40:27,798 --> 00:40:28,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Of existence.

475
00:40:28,638 --> 00:40:36,784
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not striving to find, as I said, some kind of external transcendental entity that you merge with or receives you.

476
00:40:36,864 --> 00:40:36,984
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

477
00:40:37,645 --> 00:40:47,052
[SPEAKER_01]: You're striving to stop misconceiving this very experience, to stop appropriating it as mine, stop entertaining mistaken gratuitous assumption in regard to it.

478
00:40:47,452 --> 00:40:48,813
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will undo the being.

479
00:40:49,657 --> 00:40:50,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what being is.

480
00:40:51,638 --> 00:40:53,720
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a mistake in regard to things that appear.

481
00:40:54,260 --> 00:40:59,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Mistake of ownership, mistake of the sense of self, mistake of permanence.

482
00:41:01,004 --> 00:41:09,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people say that it sounds like a kind of trying to kill yourself, you know, like a suicide, an ultimate suicide.

483
00:41:10,070 --> 00:41:11,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's the suicide of ignorance, sure.

484
00:41:12,772 --> 00:41:14,353
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the suicide of the self-view.

485
00:41:15,917 --> 00:41:21,281
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not the suicide of the content on account of which you develop the sense of self.

486
00:41:22,782 --> 00:41:30,027
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like people killing the five aggregates... trying to kill the five aggregates because they can't get rid of upādāna in regard to the pañcakhandha.

487
00:41:31,208 --> 00:41:35,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not knowing where the problem is, you're attacking it at a wrong... at the wrong place.

488
00:41:36,652 --> 00:41:39,594
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's usually what suicide is.

489
00:41:39,715 --> 00:41:42,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Attacking the body, trying to kill the body, so it will kind of kill you.

490
00:41:44,560 --> 00:41:55,368
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have undone your sense of self, then you don't care about living or killing the body, because it's not suicide then.

491
00:41:55,388 --> 00:42:02,954
[SPEAKER_01]: It's suicide when you're trying to get rid of that self, sense of self, by destroying the environment that supports it.

492
00:42:03,494 --> 00:42:06,737
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at it as the way I described it, you can't destroy the five aggregates.

493
00:42:08,837 --> 00:42:24,690
[SPEAKER_01]: You just keep changing the content of it through getting a different birth, different rebirth, which means you then can't... you don't... you don't do anything about the underlying problem of misconceiving the content that resulted in you having the self-view in the first place.

494
00:42:25,691 --> 00:42:34,317
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might have annihilated the temporary or rather the particular content, but overall, your mistake in regard to the nature of the content stays the same.

495
00:42:34,418 --> 00:42:36,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, you keep getting born

496
00:42:36,841 --> 00:42:43,806
[SPEAKER_01]: On and on and on again, and keep reappropriating things, misappropriating, misconceiving, and keep being bound by saṃsāra.

497
00:42:49,069 --> 00:43:05,400
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the true suicide, the one that can truly kill that sense of self, is the one that doesn't kill the body, doesn't aim at the body, but actually undoes, undoes the ignorance in regard to the body, in regard to the phenomenon of the body,

498
00:43:06,785 --> 00:43:27,863
[SPEAKER_01]: It's when nibbāna is annihilation of greed, aversion, delusion, of conceivings, of appropriation of the sense of self, but not annihilation of the content and of the environment and of the five aggregates.

499
00:43:31,555 --> 00:43:35,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's obviously suicide seems to be quite a bad thing.

500
00:43:36,378 --> 00:43:44,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, because it's, you see, it's just a further proliferation of already, of the original mistake.

501
00:43:45,265 --> 00:43:52,930
[SPEAKER_01]: You misconceive the content of your experience, you're not seeing the nature of it, you develop the sense of ownership and sense of self, the external self that's in charge of this whole thing.

502
00:43:54,326 --> 00:44:00,189
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, despite being paired with unpleasant feelings and so on, you still can't see that you are not in control of those things.

503
00:44:00,229 --> 00:44:14,897
[SPEAKER_01]: And instead of taking that as a way of liberating yourself from that gratuitous assumption of ownership, of control, you then try to, like, destroy the whole thing, while actually aiming at destroying that ownership and that sense of self.

504
00:44:15,938 --> 00:44:23,142
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said before, as the suttas say, the upādāna, the assumption of self or any other assumption, it's not the five aggregates.

505
00:44:24,732 --> 00:44:26,614
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not to be found apart from that.

506
00:44:27,434 --> 00:44:32,118
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to discern the nature of the five aggregates, so you stop having desire and lust in regard to the five aggregates.

507
00:44:32,499 --> 00:44:37,583
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of that, the sense of self that's being simultaneously generated by it ceases.

508
00:44:38,564 --> 00:44:41,626
[SPEAKER_01]: But people don't know that, so then they just try to destroy the problem.

509
00:44:41,966 --> 00:44:44,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Just dismiss the problem, and that's how you solve the problem.

510
00:44:44,989 --> 00:44:46,430
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why suicide is bad.

511
00:44:47,751 --> 00:44:53,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, on account of the problem, you just perpetuated the problem, but then remove the possibility of understanding the problem,

512
00:44:54,107 --> 00:44:55,368
[SPEAKER_01]: In any foreseeable future.

513
00:44:57,830 --> 00:45:01,352
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what about people who have understood the problem?

514
00:45:02,393 --> 00:45:02,913
[SPEAKER_01]: The arahants?

515
00:45:03,233 --> 00:45:03,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

516
00:45:03,774 --> 00:45:09,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, their suicide, like in the suttas, when arahants committed, it's not a suicide.

517
00:45:09,218 --> 00:45:12,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they were not aiming or trying to get rid of that sense of self.

518
00:45:12,560 --> 00:45:14,901
[SPEAKER_01]: They were not, obviously, not distraught or anything.

519
00:45:14,921 --> 00:45:15,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Depressed.

520
00:45:16,523 --> 00:45:22,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they, as the Buddha would say, he wouldn't use the word death as like they kill themselves.

521
00:45:23,612 --> 00:45:38,639
[SPEAKER_01]: You would say they would... the ones they were sick, physically sick, the body was just in this horrible falling apart state, dysentery and what not, beyond repair, beyond medicine, and they already uprooted everything in regard to that experience.

522
00:45:39,319 --> 00:45:42,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So then they just, literally, sped up.

523
00:45:45,782 --> 00:45:46,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Quicken the process.

524
00:45:46,943 --> 00:45:51,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Quicken the... that which was already coming to its end.

525
00:45:52,535 --> 00:46:04,719
[SPEAKER_01]: By freeing the sense of... freeing the assumption of the external world, freeing the... removing the notion of appropriation, the body that has still remained for an arahant, the five aggregates, are the last.

526
00:46:05,500 --> 00:46:07,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no desire and lust in regard to it.

527
00:46:07,500 --> 00:46:10,522
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no reappearance of the content anymore.

528
00:46:10,622 --> 00:46:13,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Once this body breaks apart, it will not carry on, basically.

529
00:46:14,503 --> 00:46:14,863
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

530
00:46:15,083 --> 00:46:16,584
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the true ending.

531
00:46:16,684 --> 00:46:19,945
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for an arahant, like Venerable Chana in Madhyamani Kaya,

532
00:46:20,359 --> 00:46:32,406
[SPEAKER_01]: ...who was so ill and so sick in constant discomfort, he thought, well, he has no reason to wait whether this body will break apart in five years or five days, because he'd done the work of uprooting everything.

533
00:46:32,466 --> 00:46:35,268
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing more to be done.

534
00:46:35,308 --> 00:46:35,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

535
00:46:35,608 --> 00:46:36,709
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing more to be done.

536
00:46:37,049 --> 00:46:46,655
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, because the circumstances of that body were so disagreeable, he had no reason to endure it.

537
00:46:46,815 --> 00:46:47,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would he endure it?

538
00:46:47,876 --> 00:46:49,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing to prove, nothing to do anymore.

539
00:46:50,420 --> 00:46:53,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, he's just decided to kill that body.

540
00:46:53,683 --> 00:46:58,887
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, just to, instead of waiting for five years for it to disintegrate, he'd done it in five days.

541
00:46:59,907 --> 00:47:03,830
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's a valid thing to do only after you become an arahant.

542
00:47:05,211 --> 00:47:13,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, if there is still desire and lust in regard to that dead body that you're trying to kill, means you are committing a suicide.

543
00:47:14,779 --> 00:47:16,180
[SPEAKER_01]: To a various degree, obviously.

544
00:47:17,211 --> 00:47:20,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Depending on how much desire and lust is a motivator for that action.

545
00:47:20,634 --> 00:47:22,215
[SPEAKER_01]: But for an arahant, it can't be any.

546
00:47:22,935 --> 00:47:30,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for him, it's truly just... just... just... just... fast... like speeding up the process of the disintegration of the five aggregates.

547
00:47:32,442 --> 00:47:36,165
[SPEAKER_01]: But if there is desire and lust in regards to it, they are not disintegrating.

548
00:47:37,106 --> 00:47:40,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you do remain bound with the nature of them, which means you will keep reappearing.

549
00:47:42,090 --> 00:47:44,992
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the reason why suicide is very unwholesome,

550
00:47:45,517 --> 00:48:02,569
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because by doing it, you end up binding yourself most likely with even less fortunate content of your five aggregates, which will then prevent the possibility of reflection necessary for removing the assumption and developing wisdom and understanding.

551
00:48:03,309 --> 00:48:04,530
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, the animal realm.

552
00:48:05,986 --> 00:48:21,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through your own ignorance, greed, aversion, delusion and assumptions, wrong assumptions in the sense of self, you committed acts then, that removed even further the possibility for you understanding that problem in the first place.

553
00:48:24,915 --> 00:48:31,619
[SPEAKER_01]: And which means then you have to keep enduring saṃsāra much longer, because of your own ignorant actions.

554
00:48:31,699 --> 00:48:34,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why somebody who is not an arahant, it's actually better not to

555
00:48:35,573 --> 00:48:56,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, a lot of people who are not even practicing, they've got a heavy disease, painful disease, some cancer of some kind, and there is no cure.

556
00:48:57,461 --> 00:48:57,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

557
00:48:57,841 --> 00:48:59,501
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, they're in a lot of pain.

558
00:48:59,781 --> 00:49:00,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

559
00:49:00,322 --> 00:49:02,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, they choose, say, euthanasia.

560
00:49:02,902 --> 00:49:03,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

561
00:49:03,283 --> 00:49:04,183
[SPEAKER_00]: To kill themselves.

562
00:49:05,133 --> 00:49:07,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no hope.

563
00:49:08,695 --> 00:49:08,955
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

564
00:49:09,115 --> 00:49:12,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's true that the time will come when there is no hope.

565
00:49:12,197 --> 00:49:16,619
[SPEAKER_01]: But what is not true is that that pain is the greatest pain that you can experience.

566
00:49:17,199 --> 00:49:19,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind is still affected by ignorance.

567
00:49:20,801 --> 00:49:33,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the worst pain of the human realm might not be anything in comparison to the pains of being paired with lower types of content in your future reappearances.

568
00:49:35,035 --> 00:49:38,796
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of... again, any reference you can make, it's based on this life.

569
00:49:38,836 --> 00:49:42,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And it seems unbearable, but you have actually no idea which... where you are heading.

570
00:49:43,517 --> 00:49:46,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's better to try and make absolutely most of it while you can.

571
00:49:47,277 --> 00:49:51,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you will die eventually.

572
00:49:51,078 --> 00:49:52,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to endure pain.

573
00:49:52,298 --> 00:49:53,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to endure it, yeah.

574
00:49:53,679 --> 00:49:57,800
[SPEAKER_01]: And the developed mind can endure it.

575
00:49:58,860 --> 00:49:59,960
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind did not have

576
00:50:01,195 --> 00:50:05,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Or if it has been undermined and eroded for a longer time, so it couldn't develop to the same extent.

577
00:50:06,880 --> 00:50:15,306
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, there is a serious amount of responsibility, eh... if a person commits suicide and they still have the wrong view.

578
00:50:16,407 --> 00:50:17,428
[SPEAKER_01]: They're still a kutujana.

579
00:50:18,269 --> 00:50:20,971
[SPEAKER_01]: They still haven't understood even what nature and nibbāna is.

580
00:50:22,032 --> 00:50:28,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Because... because they might be, eh... removing that possibility of understanding it.

581
00:50:29,118 --> 00:50:30,699
[SPEAKER_01]: They remove it for a long, long time.

582
00:50:31,401 --> 00:50:44,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if the... if the next... next births and rebirths and reappearances, whatever you want to call it, are lower than the human realm, or even if they are higher, if it's just infused with divine pleasures, you won't be thinking and reflecting much.

583
00:50:46,435 --> 00:50:54,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you really want to make the most of this... this opportunity that you have, where you can reflect with that right balance, which is what human realm is.

584
00:50:57,064 --> 00:50:58,045
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you've done that work,

585
00:50:58,682 --> 00:51:05,927
[SPEAKER_01]: If you fully free the whole experience of the five areas, there's no external assumptions, no conceivings, there is no suicide, there is no murder, there is no death.

586
00:51:07,528 --> 00:51:27,502
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is something that everyone should be training towards, because everyone is going some some way or... Yeah, ultimately, that should be... that's pretty much the soul... the aim of any being in any realm should be freeing themselves from being bound with that... with the content of their experience, with the saṃsāra.

587
00:51:28,931 --> 00:51:33,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Just in terms of enduring pain, painful feeling.

588
00:51:33,133 --> 00:51:39,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's like the beginning of restraint as well.

589
00:51:39,596 --> 00:51:41,517
[SPEAKER_01]: While you're healthy, you start practicing that.

590
00:51:42,998 --> 00:51:50,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when the senses start falling apart, if you haven't practiced restraint and enduring, you won't be able to...

